---
// The builds are ad-hoc signed, not notarized, so macOS blocks the first launch.
// Say so on the download page: someone who hits an unexplained refusal assumes
// the app is broken and leaves. Mirrors the README section.
---

<aside class="note">
  <h3>First launch on macOS</h3>
  <p>
    These builds aren&rsquo;t signed with a paid Apple Developer certificate, so
    macOS blocks the first launch and says it can&rsquo;t verify the developer.
    Let it through once and later launches are normal:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Double-click the app. macOS offers <em>Move to Trash</em> or
      <em>Done</em> — press <strong>Done</strong>. This step is required: it is
      what makes the button in step&nbsp;3 appear.
    </li>
    <li>
      Open <strong>System Settings &rsaquo; Privacy &amp; Security</strong> and
      scroll to <em>Security</em>.
    </li>
    <li>Next to the note about the app, click <strong>Open Anyway</strong>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p class="terminal">Or, from a terminal, run the command for the app you installed:</p>
  <pre><code>xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine "/Applications/ROT Reader.app"
xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine "/Applications/ROT Pad.app"</code></pre>
</aside>

